hi my name is Adam Carrington and I'm an  assistant professor of politics here at  Hillsdale College and I'd like to ask  the question can the police arrest you  for speaking freely for exercising your  right to free speech that question  actually came up in the court this week  in avis versus Bartlett Bartlett was  attending a function called Arctic man  up in Alaska and got into an altercation  with with several Alaskan police  officers they arrested him he argues  that they did so to retaliate against  certain things he said and refused to  say to them and so the question the  court had to take up is a balance of two  principles on one hand we can't have the  government arresting you to retaliate  for things you believe and things that  you've expressed for example what if you  believe something about abortion or  immigration or local zoning ordinances  that police don't agree with can they  arrest you and the answer is no but on  the other side police have to make often  snap judgments to protect us and if we  were to not allow them some discretion  in arresting or not arresting persons we  would have very little law enforcement  and therefore very little protection of  our life liberty and property given that  how is the court to balance the two I  think the majority while getting the  vote right voting with the officers that  they were allowed to do what they did  still got the principle a little bit off  on one hand they gave a blanket  protection to the police if the police  could show that they had probable cause  for the arrest now I think probable  cause should play a role I think some of  the other opinions some of the  concurrences particularly by Gorsuch and  Ruth Bader Ginsburg pointed out that  this shouldn't be a blanket protection  instead probable call should be one  factor to show that police were not  arresting you because they didn't like  what you said and in doing so what  someone like Gorsuch in particular  recognizes is that free speech rights  aren't the same as Fourth Amendment  rights  amendment rights protect you against  being arrested for no reason but you can  be arrested for a decent reason and  arrested because your speaking things  the government doesn't want you to say  Gorsuch is opinion and I would say  Ginsburg's as well sets up a more  nuanced standard that allows protection  to police officers so that they can  carry out their job but at the same time  gives protection to free speech rights  as free speech rights for the protection  of our Liberty in general
 

